name 'csaif_pl_ai_01-rev-02'
title 'Ethics of AI Collaboration'
description ''
question 'Your students are debating the question: **Who should own AI-generated content—the AI creator, the user, the company, or shared ownership? And what happens if AI is trained on copyrighted material?** During the discussion, several students strongly assert their opinions, while others stay quiet or seem unsure. You want students to explore multiple perspectives, consider trade-offs, and understand that ethical questions can have many valid viewpoints. Which teacher move would best support a productive classroom discussion that surfaces diverse viewpoints and encourages thoughtful reasoning?'

wrong 'Tell the class the correct answer so students know the official ethical position and can move on.'
wrong  'Allow students to continue to opt in or out of the discussion, since this is a topic that might make some students uncomfortable.'
right 'Invite students to articulate different stakeholder perspectives (e.g., artists, companies, users, lawmakers), and ask follow-up questions that prompt them to explain the values behind each viewpoint.'
wrong 'Redirect the conversation to a different topic to avoid potential disagreements or discomfort.'
allow_multiple_attempts true